[[:db/retract 17592186204266 :question/answer-fields 17592186200360] [:db/retract 17592186204266 :question/answer-fields 17592186200354] [:db/retract 17592186204266 :question/answer-fields 17592186200363] [:db/retract 17592186204266 :question/answer-fields 17592186200357] [:db/retract 17592186204265 :question/answer-fields 17592186200336] [:db/retract 17592186204265 :question/answer-fields 17592186200342] [:db/retract 17592186204265 :question/answer-fields 17592186200345] [:db/retract 17592186204265 :question/answer-fields 17592186200348] [:db/retract 17592186204265 :question/answer-fields 17592186200333] {:db/id 17592186203707 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A = \\{\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (\\heartsuit,\\heartsuit), (\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit), (\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit), (\\spadesuit,\\spadesuit), (\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit), (\\diamondsuit, \\diamondsuit) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the relation $R$ is transitive or not." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z{:}$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit)$ whose second component is $\\spadesuit.$ It can be linked with the following pair from $R$ whose first component is $\\spadesuit{:}$ \n\n- Linking $(\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit)$ with $(\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit)$ requires that $(\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit) \\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit)$ whose second component is $\\diamondsuit.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $\\diamondsuit$ and a second component other than $\\diamondsuit.$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit)$ whose second component is $\\diamondsuit.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $\\diamondsuit$ and a second component other than $\\diamondsuit.$\n\nSo, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is transitive." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-44cd3428-3b1c-5e19-9525-5e86c3ca616a" "question-202c6abf-dfb5-522a-9256-456835ce12bd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203718 :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A = \\{\\alpha, \\beta, \\delta\\}$ are transitive?\n\n- $R_1 = A^2$\n\n- $R_2 = \\{(\\alpha,\\beta), (\\alpha,\\delta)\\}$\n\n- $R_3 = \\{(\\beta,\\alpha), (\\beta,\\delta), (\\delta, \\beta)\\}$\n\n- $R_4 = \\{(\\alpha,\\alpha), (\\beta,\\beta), (\\delta, \\delta)\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z{:}$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R = A^2$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R = \\emptyset$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- $R_1$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $R_2$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $R_3$ is *not* transitive. Linking $(\\beta,\\delta)$ with $(\\delta, \\beta)$ requires that $(\\beta,\\beta) \\in R_3.$ However, this pair is *not* in $R_3.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- $R_4$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203887 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-93be41a4-83b1-5ac1-a124-d1424d516f32" "question-64ea6724-f6be-5851-a399-860e0e1924d8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203708 :question/problem "Which of the following digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A = \\{1,2,3\\}?$\n\n![](images/f4/f4ad23f3c7794456a4cc01d4f801bfe4217605c5e17f1f612c5955be6bcaf6e6.png)" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z,$ then the arrow $x \\rightarrow z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a transitive relation. There are no pairs of vertices for which $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z.$\n\n- Digraph II does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $1 \\rightarrow 2$ and $2 \\rightarrow 1,$ but has no arrow $1 \\rightarrow 1.$\n\n- Digraph III represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z,$ the arrow  $x \\rightarrow z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-55431de3-ba18-5b2d-9929-1fa00aa2cc43" "question-70eb9024-0964-59ad-a103-f6948d25a78c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203709 :question/problem "Which of the following relations are transitive? \n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\not\\mid y,$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $x \\: S \\: y$ if and only if $x = y,$ defined on $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $x \\: T \\: y$ if and only if $6 \\mid (x+y),$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,3) \\in R$ since $2 \\not\\mid 3,$ and\n\n- $(3,4) \\in R$ since $3 \\not\\mid 4,$ but\n\n- $(2,4) \\notin R$ since $2 \\mid 4.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y) \\in S$ and $(y,x) \\in S,$ then $(x,z) \\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx = y \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = z \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = z\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,4) \\in T$ since $6 \\mid (2+4),$ and\n\n- $(4,8) \\in T$ since $6 \\mid (4+8),$ but\n\n- $(2,8) \\notin T$ since $6 \\not\\mid (2+8).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204266 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,2),(3,3)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\n$\\in R$ and \n{{field-2}}\n$\\in R,$ but \n{{field-3}}\n$\\notin R,$ the relation is\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,2)$ whose second component is $2.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $2:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,1)$ requires that $(1,1)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,3)$ requires that $(1,3)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,3)$ whose second component is $3.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $3:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,3)$ with $(3,2)$ requires that $(1,2)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,1)$ whose second component is $1.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $1:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,1)$ with $(1,2)$ requires that $(2,2)\\in R.$ However, this pair is *not* in $R.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nWe can stop checking since we have found a chain of pairs that do not satisfy the transitivity condition. Therefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(2,1)\\in R$ and $(1,2)\\in R$ but $(2,2)\\notin R,$ the relation is  $\\text{not transitive}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf8c9528-4b68-5e94-9cc1-f08494869ea5" "answer-field-8f053f47-0fb2-59a6-a9af-f0fec0e41d77" "answer-field-23946d69-9f9f-5316-a125-b5686b969fb7" "answer-field-ee888080-eb0a-5b7f-97aa-81b36e4d160c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf8c9528-4b68-5e94-9cc1-f08494869ea5" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8c9528-4b68-5e94-9cc1-f08494869ea5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-4e0858b5-c929-5bd0-afda-32b8314adc43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cad2ecc4-a926-5813-a1cb-308c6ba13227" "answer-4e0858b5-c929-5bd0-afda-32b8314adc43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cad2ecc4-a926-5813-a1cb-308c6ba13227" :answer/id #uuid "cad2ecc4-a926-5813-a1cb-308c6ba13227" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e0858b5-c929-5bd0-afda-32b8314adc43" :answer/id #uuid "4e0858b5-c929-5bd0-afda-32b8314adc43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f053f47-0fb2-59a6-a9af-f0fec0e41d77" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f053f47-0fb2-59a6-a9af-f0fec0e41d77" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-59f773e3-8450-5f0d-8604-aafc7e95bffc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-59f773e3-8450-5f0d-8604-aafc7e95bffc" "answer-01d07d82-bd5f-595d-b025-e9ac237f4946"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59f773e3-8450-5f0d-8604-aafc7e95bffc" :answer/id #uuid "59f773e3-8450-5f0d-8604-aafc7e95bffc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01d07d82-bd5f-595d-b025-e9ac237f4946" :answer/id #uuid "01d07d82-bd5f-595d-b025-e9ac237f4946" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-23946d69-9f9f-5316-a125-b5686b969fb7" :answer-field/id #uuid "23946d69-9f9f-5316-a125-b5686b969fb7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-11527144-b995-5941-ba03-c4baf29af809" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a240eaa0-782f-5f23-ae2b-03fdf9beab76" "answer-11527144-b995-5941-ba03-c4baf29af809"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a240eaa0-782f-5f23-ae2b-03fdf9beab76" :answer/id #uuid "a240eaa0-782f-5f23-ae2b-03fdf9beab76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11527144-b995-5941-ba03-c4baf29af809" :answer/id #uuid "11527144-b995-5941-ba03-c4baf29af809" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee888080-eb0a-5b7f-97aa-81b36e4d160c" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee888080-eb0a-5b7f-97aa-81b36e4d160c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-d2acfe4c-3493-5b61-80ea-d1e232568847" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2acfe4c-3493-5b61-80ea-d1e232568847" "answer-c4cd3338-7c8f-57cd-acd4-a846dbc5dc7b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2acfe4c-3493-5b61-80ea-d1e232568847" :answer/id #uuid "d2acfe4c-3493-5b61-80ea-d1e232568847" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not transitive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4cd3338-7c8f-57cd-acd4-a846dbc5dc7b" :answer/id #uuid "c4cd3338-7c8f-57cd-acd4-a846dbc5dc7b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "transitive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204265 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={2,4,8}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(2,4),(2,8),(4,2),(4,4),(4,8)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nIn this relation, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y){{field-1}}R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z){{field-2}}R\\,⟹\\,{{field-3}}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is \n{{field-4}}.\n\nFor example, since $(2,4)$ and $(4,8)$ belong to $R,$ transitivity of $R$ requires \n{{field-5}}\n$\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,4)$ whose second component is $4.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $4:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,4)$ with $(4,2)$ requires that $(2,2)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(2,4)$ with $(4,8)$ requires that $(2,8)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,8)$ whose second component is $8.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $8.$\n\n- Consider the pair $(4,2)$ whose second component is $2.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $2:$ \n\n- Linking $(4,2)$ with $(2,4)$ requires that $(4,4)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(4,2)$ with $(2,8)$ requires that $(4,8)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(4,8)$ whose second component is $8.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $8.$\n\nSo, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is  $\\text{transitive}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-128eeed9-769b-58c6-9e3f-3255a669e8b9" "answer-field-19b909e7-859c-5d54-9d15-aec04dd7113f" "answer-field-18473a2f-39a3-525e-b6d3-37aafd0009ed" "answer-field-0503ba64-6251-5d0b-868d-6f53851f7698" "answe