ive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ef4800a4-4dd7-502d-8ed6-b7f58d1fc8fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4800a4-4dd7-502d-8ed6-b7f58d1fc8fb" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b7d20a5c-de38-52bd-a2e1-c3df98b7b782" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36d738e0-5f31-50b1-8918-aba63fd1c1c7" "answer-b7d20a5c-de38-52bd-a2e1-c3df98b7b782" "answer-73560a46-39e0-5eb3-85e9-338e3894a509" "answer-3144fdfd-1612-56e5-a89f-1e8bc6c495fb" "answer-882d8c7f-a581-579f-a530-de35b09f833c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36d738e0-5f31-50b1-8918-aba63fd1c1c7" :answer/id #uuid "36d738e0-5f31-50b1-8918-aba63fd1c1c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7d20a5c-de38-52bd-a2e1-c3df98b7b782" :answer/id #uuid "b7d20a5c-de38-52bd-a2e1-c3df98b7b782" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73560a46-39e0-5eb3-85e9-338e3894a509" :answer/id #uuid "73560a46-39e0-5eb3-85e9-338e3894a509" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3144fdfd-1612-56e5-a89f-1e8bc6c495fb" :answer/id #uuid "3144fdfd-1612-56e5-a89f-1e8bc6c495fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(8,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-882d8c7f-a581-579f-a530-de35b09f833c" :answer/id #uuid "882d8c7f-a581-579f-a530-de35b09f833c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(8,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-44cd3428-3b1c-5e19-9525-5e86c3ca616a" :question/id #uuid "44cd3428-3b1c-5e19-9525-5e86c3ca616a" :question/math-academy-id "q-214431" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={♢,♡,♠,♣}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}={(♢,♢),(♣,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{2}={(♠,♠)}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(♢,♠),(♡,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{4}=∅$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0ba6de5b-5a73-5a97-aff3-85fcde63e486"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0ba6de5b-5a73-5a97-aff3-85fcde63e486" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ba6de5b-5a73-5a97-aff3-85fcde63e486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62c920c8-4114-5cee-ab28-e34c74683438" "answer-bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" "answer-344c8bff-5d19-596d-a2bf-472abe96d0ef" "answer-5b9a150a-cc66-599e-963e-2b2c4bed2295" "answer-b26882ef-7ad2-53e9-a273-98e2183e198c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62c920c8-4114-5cee-ab28-e34c74683438" :answer/id #uuid "62c920c8-4114-5cee-ab28-e34c74683438" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer/id #uuid "bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-344c8bff-5d19-596d-a2bf-472abe96d0ef" :answer/id #uuid "344c8bff-5d19-596d-a2bf-472abe96d0ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b9a150a-cc66-599e-963e-2b2c4bed2295" :answer/id #uuid "5b9a150a-cc66-599e-963e-2b2c4bed2295" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b26882ef-7ad2-53e9-a273-98e2183e198c" :answer/id #uuid "b26882ef-7ad2-53e9-a273-98e2183e198c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-202c6abf-dfb5-522a-9256-456835ce12bd" :question/id #uuid "202c6abf-dfb5-522a-9256-456835ce12bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-214430" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={1,3,5,7}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}=∅$\n- ${R}_{2}={A}^{2}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(5,5)}$\n- ${R}_{4}={(5,1),(7,3)}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c8488c7a-8619-58cd-a22e-a5aa6766ebf6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c8488c7a-8619-58cd-a22e-a5aa6766ebf6" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8488c7a-8619-58cd-a22e-a5aa6766ebf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db2ad807-5ae0-5e39-8f4e-ad108c836dcd" "answer-2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" "answer-ac9e3534-044f-5762-90a9-b43cb8e6b482" "answer-2571c089-3c52-59d7-b473-6f9e0305f21e" "answer-cf9cc960-4433-54c6-935f-f6a98ffc3777"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db2ad807-5ae0-5e39-8f4e-ad108c836dcd" :answer/id #uuid "db2ad807-5ae0-5e39-8f4e-ad108c836dcd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer/id #uuid "2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac9e3534-044f-5762-90a9-b43cb8e6b482" :answer/id #uuid "ac9e3534-044f-5762-90a9-b43cb8e6b482" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2571c089-3c52-59d7-b473-6f9e0305f21e" :answer/id #uuid "2571c089-3c52-59d7-b473-6f9e0305f21e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf9cc960-4433-54c6-935f-f6a98ffc3777" :answer/id #uuid "cf9cc960-4433-54c6-935f-f6a98ffc3777" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-93be41a4-83b1-5ac1-a124-d1424d516f32" :question/id #uuid "93be41a4-83b1-5ac1-a124-d1424d516f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-214053" :question/problem "![](images/20/2040dcca071aff2235916003d656ad12a9be20ae840124ae2152e5c48b4cccb1.png)\n\nA binary relation is represented by the digraph shown above. Select the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince the digraph has arrows\n{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}\nbut has no arrow\n{{field-3}},\nthe relation is \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nExamining the diagram, we conclude the following:\n\nSince the digraph has arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 4$ but has no arrow $1\\to 4,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e33ceb2-527d-539c-aac7-2b5c809de84e" "answer-field-c25dd648-07a4-5a0f-b4ab-b34bf8cdb08b" "answer-field-3cbb7d3e-9fb9-5ba5-8426-510de8de7d0c" "answer-field-890dfe13-7d09-5994-b52b-2c52a0c3aae5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e33ceb2-527d-539c-aac7-2b5c809de84e" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e33ceb2-527d-539c-aac7-2b5c809de84e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-4e1c3e39-8891-537f-a591-b967bd41d83a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e1c3e39-8891-537f-a591-b967bd41d83a" "answer-2eb95cc2-4351-5219-bf66-5f1e95ed104c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e1c3e39-8891-537f-a591-b967bd41d83a" :answer/id #uuid "4e1c3e39-8891-537f-a591-b967bd41d83a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\to 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eb95cc2-4351-5219-bf66-5f1e95ed104c" :answer/id #uuid "2eb95cc2-4351-5219-bf66-5f1e95ed104c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\to 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c25dd648-07a4-5a0f-b4ab-b34bf8cdb08b" :answer-field/id #uuid "c25dd648-07a4-5a0f-b4ab-b34bf8cdb08b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-43ed7d0b-7555-52b8-974e-fff2e2e26faa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43ed7d0b-7555-52b8-974e-fff2e2e26faa" "answer-2a370020-7fc9-5273-b46a-bc1fe9f1427c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43ed7d0b-7555-52b8-974e-fff2e2e26faa" :answer/id #uuid "43ed7d0b-7555-52b8-974e-fff2e2e26faa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\to 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a370020-7fc9-5273-b46a-bc1fe9f1427c" :answer/id #uuid "2a370020-7fc9-5273-b46a-bc1fe9f1427c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\to 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cbb7d3e-9fb9-5ba5-8426-510de8de7d0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cbb7d3e-9fb9-5ba5-8426-510de8de7d0c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-df66e2d9-8d32-51de-a62f-82de35f4f3d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df66e2d9-8d32-51de-a62f-82de35f4f3d2" "answer-b6037192-7f93-5c5f-b0b2-e7993fdde29a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df66e2d9-8d32-51de-a62f-82de35f4f3d2" :answer/id #uuid "df66e2d9-8d32-51de-a62f-82de35f4f3d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\to 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6037192-7f93-5c5f-b0b2-e7993fdde29a" :answer/id #uuid "b6037192-7f93-5c5f-b0b2-e7993fdde29a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\to 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-890dfe13-7d09-5994-b52b-2c52a0c3aae5" :answer-field/id #uuid "890dfe13-7d09-5994-b52b-2c52a0c3aae5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-899fb552-6af3-5ceb-bf2a-62dbe05b5878" :answer-field/choices ["answer-899fb552-6af3-5ceb-bf2a-62dbe05b5878" "answer-a969fef6-bbaa-530c-8fd5-40194ddef068"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-899fb552-6af3-5ceb-bf2a-62dbe05b5878" :answer/id #uuid "899fb552-6af3-5ceb-bf2a-62dbe05b5878" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not transitive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a969fef6-bbaa-530c-8fd5-40194ddef068" :answer/id #uuid "a969fef6-bbaa-530c-8fd5-40194ddef068" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "transitive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-64ea6724-f6be-5851-a399-860e0e1924d8" :question/id #uuid "64ea6724-f6be-5851-a399-860e0e1924d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-214065" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fa323b54736f62bb334ae491d8d41deeba4a4e325f6e56f9651da147d50c349.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A={p,q,r}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $q\\to r$ and $r\\to p,$ but has no arrow $q\\to p.$\n\n- Digraph II represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ the arrow  $x\\to z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $p\\to q$ and $q\\to r,$ but has no arrow $p\\to r.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-18915ff6-afcd-56a1-8b11-45e467d4f091"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-18915ff6-afcd-56a1-8b11-45e467d4f091" :answer-field/id #uuid "18915ff6-afcd-56a1-8b11-45e467d4f091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9c59538-8b1e-5b20-9482-690ba54c57e0" "answer-81acb1cb-213f-53e4-b5af-c60b8dbbec53" "answer-c5c12167-746e-5442-b291-b883d8a00d11" "answer-9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" "answer-4e69dd30-577b-5eb0-8abc-73d0336ecd72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9c59538-8b1e-5b20-9482-690ba54c57e0" :answer/id #uuid "a9c59538-8b1e-5b20-9482-690ba54c57e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81acb1cb-213f-53e4-b5af-c60b8dbbec53" :answer/id #uuid "81acb1cb-213f-53e4-b5af-c60b8dbbec53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5c12167-746e-5442-b291-b883d8a00d11" :answer/id #uuid "c5c12167-746e-5442-b291-b883d8a00d11" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer/id #uuid "9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e69dd30-577b-5eb0-8abc-73d0336ecd72" :answer/id #uuid "4e69dd30-577b-5eb0-8abc-73d0336ecd72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-55431de3-ba18-5b2d-9929-1fa00aa2cc43" :question/id #uuid "55431de3-ba18-5b2d-9929-1fa00aa2cc43" :question/math-academy-id "q-213994" :question/problem "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x+y=1$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}},\nbut \n{{field-3}},\nthe relation is \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nFor our relation, notice that\n\n- $(-2,3)\\in R$ since $-2+3=1,$ and\n\n- $(3,-2)\\in R$ since $3+(-2)=1,$ but\n\n- $(-2,-2)\\notin R$ since $-2+(-2)\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(-2,3)\\in R$ and $(3,-2)\\in R$ but \n$(-2,-2)\\notin R,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6d06f9b-e36a-58c5-96a2-91bce44b80cf" "answer-field-1f1e0f09-949f-54d6-931b-a8acdad7849c" "answer-field-c438305c-c60f-5ca3-9fe4-64720823e89a" "answer-field-4eda63be-0db3-597c-99cd-8d04a3169f0d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6d06f9b-e36a-58c5-96a2-91bce44b80cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d06f9b-e36a-58c5-96a2-91bce44b80cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-0c81ee74-4165-534e-af15-27d266abcfea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6041ca23-bc67-5932-802e-ab03ef5d1b6b" "answer-0c81ee74-4165-534e-af15-27d266abcfea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6041ca23-bc67-5932-802e-ab03ef5d1b6b" :answer/id #uuid "6041ca23-bc67-5932-802e-ab03ef5d1b6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,2)\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c81ee74-4165-534e-af15-27d266abcfea" :answer/id #uuid "0c81ee74-4165-534e-af15-27d266abcfea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,3)\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f1e0f09-949f-54d6-931b-a8acdad7849c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1e0f09-949f-54d6-931b-a8acdad7849c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-f933ce82-e344-5cf6-a182-c384a6e0a795" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2958ba5-f4a7-5209-8df3-1f6cb1c7c4c5" "answer-f933ce82-e344-5cf6-a182-c384a6e0a795"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2958ba5-f4a7-5209-8df3-1f6cb1c7c4c5" :answer/id #uuid "c2958ba5-f4a7-5209-8df3-1f6cb1c7c4c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,1)\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f933ce82-e344-5cf6-a182-c384a6e0a795" :answer/id #uuid "f933ce82-e344-5cf6-a182-c384a6e0a795" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-2)\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c438305c-c60f-5ca3-9fe4-64720823e89a" :answer-field/id #uuid "c438305c-c60f-5ca3-9fe4-64720823e89a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-949b1d87-d93b-501e-aa04-48eb488ae07a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20f9e528-a322-5fab-b404-6979d866a63b" "answer-43b1f671-c4ee-5c91-9ebe-8a00a2ddd2c7" "answer-35e7dbdc-f004-543a-93fd-daec3d693af1" "answer-949b1d87-d93b-501e-aa04-48eb488ae07a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20f9e528-a322-5fab-b404-6979d866a63b" :answer/id #uuid "20f9e528-a322-5fab-b404-6979d866a63b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43b1f671-c4ee-5c91-9ebe-8a00a2ddd2c7" :answer/id #uuid "43b1f671-c4ee-5c91-9ebe-8a00a2ddd2c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)\\notin R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35e7dbdc-f004-543a-93fd-daec3d693af1" :answer/id #uuid "35e7dbdc-f004-543a-93fd-daec3d693af1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-2)\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-949b1d87-d93b-501e-aa04-48eb488ae07a" :answer/id #uuid "949b1d87-d93b-501e-aa04-48eb488ae07a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-2)\\notin R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4eda63be-0db3-597c-99cd-8d04a3169f0d" :answer-field/id #uuid "4eda63be-0db3-597c-99cd-8d04a3169f0d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9d312a08-bec8-54ed-928e-fb44841278d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d312a08-bec8-54ed-928e-fb44841278d5" "answer-0f2f48f7-907c-5201-9e82-8318cf32ee9f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d312a08-bec8-54ed-928e-fb44841278d5" :answer/id #uuid "9d312a08-bec8-54ed-928e-fb44841278d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not transitive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f2f48f7-907c-5201-9e82-8318cf32ee9f" :answer/id #uuid "0f2f48f7-907c-5201-9e82-8318cf32ee9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "transitive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-70eb9024-0964-59ad-a103-f6948d25a78c" :question/id #uuid "70eb9024-0964-59ad-a103-f6948d25a78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214002" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are transitive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y<4,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,x)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx∣y\\,\\text{and}\\,y∣z\\,⟹\\,x∣z\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,-4)\\in S$ since $1+(-4)=-3<4,$ and\n\n- $(-4,5)\\in S$ since $-4+5=1<4,$ but\n\n- $(1,5)\\notin S$ since $1+5=6≮4.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ and $(y,x)\\in T,$ then $(x,z)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,\\text{and}\\,y≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟹\\,x≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $T$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-edb2118f-fc60-559a-b8bd-52ea337c03d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-edb2118f-fc60-559a-b8bd-52ea337c03d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "edb2118f-fc60-559a-b8bd-52ea337c03d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" "answer-e564fc12-fb2c-519f-9637-195e019015f7" "answer-9c5a523e-87ad-564c-a53c-0f5976b548cd" "answer-c19f5713-624c-5de7-b871-ec4d7fff4f4f" "answer-c9cc9a32-4374-5c62-b18b-114a993cc029"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer/id #uuid "e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e564fc12-fb2c-519f-9637-195e019015f7" :answer/id #uuid "e564fc12-fb2c-519f-9637-195e019015f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c5a523e-87ad-564c-a53c-0f5976b548cd" :answer/id #uuid "9c5a523e-87ad-564c-a53c-0f5976b548cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c19f5713-624c-5de7-b871-ec4d7fff4f4f" :answer/id #uuid "c19f5713-624c-5de7-b871-ec4d7fff4f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9cc9a32-4374-5c62-b18b-114a993cc029" :answer/id #uuid "c9cc9a32-4374-5c62-b18b-114a993cc029" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205242 :tutorial/content "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is **transitive** if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y}) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y})$ and $(\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z})$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z})$ should also belong to the relation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The relation \"greater than\" ($\\gt$), defined over $\\mathbb{R},$ is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\n- The relation \"are congruent modulo $n$\" ($\\equiv$), defined over $\\mathbb{Z},$ is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ where $n$ is a fixed natural number.\n\nNotice from the definition of a transitive relation that if the first ordered pair  is $(y,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n(y,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (y,z) \\in R\n$$\n\nwhich is always true.\n\nSimilarly, if the second ordered pair is $(y,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y) \\in R\n$$\n\nwhich is always true. \n\nTherefore, to verify transitivity, it's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z.$\n\nLet's see some examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205248 :tutorial/content "Let's consider some \"nuanced\" cases.\n\n- For any set $A,$ the universal relation $R = A^2$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R = \\emptyset$ is transitive. Since it does not contain any pairs, the antecedent in the implication from the definition of transitivity is always false:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R}_{\\large \\textrm{false}} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R\n$$\n\nTherefore, the implication is always true, and the relation is vacuously transitive.\n\n- The relation $R = \\{(1,2), (3,4)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs. So, the antecedent in the implication is always false (similar to the empty case above), and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- The relation $R=\\{(1,1), (2,2)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs (i.e., it contains only reflexive pairs)." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205253 :tutorial/content "A binary relation is transitive if and only if for every pair of \"linked\" arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z$ in the corresponding digraph, the arrow $x \\rightarrow z$ also belongs to the graph. For example, the relation represented by the following digraph is transitive. \n\n![](images/96/96fe8200e2ebd03c23ade6b649a41345f75b66524c188d419ee12ea3c904942b.png)\n\n \n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" arrows of the form $x \\to y$ with $x \\neq y$ and $y \\to z$ with $y \\neq z{:}$\n\n- Consider the arrow $1 \\to 2$ that ends at $2.$ It can be linked with the following arrows that starts at $2{:}$ \n\n- Linking $1 \\to 2$ with $2 \\to 1$ requires that $1 \\to 1$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Linking $1 \\to 2$ with $2 \\to 3$ requires that $1 \\to 3$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Consider the arrow $2 \\to 1$ that ends at $1.$ It can be linked with the following arrows that starts at $1{:}$ \n\n- Linking $2 \\to 1$ with $1 \\to 2$ requires that $2 \\to 2$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Linking $2 \\to 1$ with $1 \\to 3$ requires that $2 \\to 3$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Consider the arrow $1 \\to 3$ that ends at $3.$ It can't be linked with any arrow since none of them start at $3.$\n\n- Consider the arrow $2 \\to 3$ that ends at $3.$ It can't be linked with any arrow since none of them start at $3.$\n\nOn the other hand, the relation that is represented by the digraph below is not transitive since the digraph has arrows $1 \\to 2$ and $2 \\to 4$ but has no arrow $1 \\to 4.$\n\n![](images/04/04ade4abe648bcbd790c6355461fb3f74184667a0a6f4fbbafa8b84859c8c28f.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204114 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
